/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 226 0 R
/E (The evolution of automated form-filling has transitioned from basic optical character recognition and static rule-based systems to sophisticated multimodal document intelligence. This section provides a technical analysis of existing research and establishes the comparative necessity of the IVAN AI framework.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 226 0 R
/E (Analysis of Previous Works: Research in accessibility-focused automation often prioritizes specific interaction modalities. Hegde et al. [6] introduced the Dhvani voice Bot, utilizing the RASA framework and Knowledge Graphs to assist monolingual Kannada speakers with government schemes. While achieving 97% intent accuracy, the system is constrained by its lack of document processing integration and reliance on a single regional language. In contrast, multimodal efforts like those by Syed [29] explore the integration of voice and image recognition, yet often lack a cohesive pipeline for the linguistic diversity of the Indian subcontinent. The Vistaar benchmark [27] further underscores this gap, revealing that while ASR for 59 Indian languages is maturing with reduced Word Error Rates \(WER\), its integration into downstream automation workflows remains largely unexplored.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 226 0 R
/E (The domain of Optical Character Recognition \(OCR\) and Information Extraction \(IE\) has seen significant attempts at handling structured data. Traditional pipelines [13], [15], [30] have focused on identity documents like Aadhaar and PAN cards, utilizing PyTesseract or Google Vision APIs for direct field extraction from images. However, some of these systems often rely on static Regex-based methods of IE and post-processing that fail under the noise of poor-quality scans, stamps or complex varying layouts [12]. Lat and Jawahar [7] addressed some of these concerns through Deep Learning based preprocessing using their Super-Resolution framework, achieving a 21.19% accuracy gain, but their work remains English-centric and sensitive to cluttered layouts. Recent benchmarks such as Clutter OCR Bench [12] and PreP-OCR [11] highlight that even state-of-the-art models suffer from high Character Error rates \(CER\) of over 55% when faced with physical complexities and clutters like ink spills or food stains, complex layout distortions and most specifically code-mixed scripts common in real-world governmental documents.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 226 0 R
/T (The rise of Vision Language Models \(VLMs\) and Generative Information Extraction \(GIE\) [17] has introduced layout-aware understanding. Modern architectures like SCAN [10] and zero-shot Table-of-Contents guided RAG frameworks [20] outperform traditional OCR in spatial reasoning and textual retrieval. However, as noted in FormFactory [26] and surveys by Ghosh et al. [19], these models act as "black-boxes" with high computational overhead, making them unsuitable for edge deployment. Semantic document analysis using Graph)
/E (The rise of Vision Language Models \(VLMs\) and Generative Information Extraction \(GIE\) [17] has introduced layout-aware understanding. Modern architectures like SCAN [10] and zero-shot Table-of-Contents guided RAG frameworks [20] outperform traditional OCR in spatial reasoning and textual retrieval. However, as noted in FormFactory [26] and surveys by Ghosh et al. [19], these models act as "black-boxes" with high computational overhead, making them unsuitable for edge deployment. Semantic document analysis using Graph)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
